A||||8||87FieroGTx||Detailing Engine bay??||||1||
Z||000000||87FieroGTx||06-28-2001||01:42 PM|||| I'm going to detail my engine bay while I wait for my new Brakes.<P> I've cleaned it all out but all the wires and hoses look dirty is there any way to shine them up?<P> A friend told me Vaseline would work, Does it?||||reg||1
Z||000001||DXR_DAD||06-28-2001||01:49 PM||||Do not use vaseline!!!!  Vaseline = DUST!!!<P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||4
Z||000002||Alex4mula||06-28-2001||02:00 PM||||I use hand cleaner (Goop) with success to clean grease out of electrical and plug wires. Just need a lot of rags or paper towel to change from dirty to clean. Other option are wire looms to cover the wors ones. I use Armorall or similar on some rubber hoses. Also braided covering looks good.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/p8cd6608274de9501de9b32aa3e00ec24/fe6d7726.jpg.orig.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>Alex4mula<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/p69fbfe3e99ec5a0b81c10afcc578ff95/fe6d5c42.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||deceler8||06-28-2001||03:13 PM||||Someone else on the forum suggested this and it works...<P>Use an engine cleaner, allow the engine to dry, and while it's still warm, spray down the engine compartment with Armorall or Sun of a Gun.<P>Works surprisingly well.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/deceler8_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||AkursedX||06-28-2001||03:29 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by deceler8:<BR><B>Someone else on the forum suggested this and it works...<P>Use an engine cleaner, allow the engine to dry, and while it's still warm, spray down the engine compartment with Armorall or Sun of a Gun.<P>Works surprisingly well.<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's what I do.  Makes all the black nice and shiney.  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P6180015_1.JPG"> <BR>This pic was taken about a week after the detailing, so it's not perfect.<P><BR>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by AkursedX (edited 06-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||beachbomb||06-28-2001||06:25 PM||||Those are nice looking engines!!!!!!! Jim(2 '84 Indys)||||reg||7
Z||000006||rogergarrison||06-28-2001||07:08 PM||||U guys all need to throw the armour all away, it causes nothing but problems. Gotta be carefull when you spray it down with something shiny. I did once, the very next day my TPS, Map sensor both quit. May have been coincedence but thats way it happened.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ronnie||06-28-2001||07:57 PM||||I use WD40 after the initial washing. It does a real good job, plus, it actually displaces water. I have used it inside distributor caps before to displace moisture.Plus it shines up the rubber and painted surfaces.||||reg||6
Z||000008||AkursedX||06-28-2001||09:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>U guys all need to throw the armour all away, it causes nothing but problems. Gotta be carefull when you spray it down with something shiny. I did once, the very next day my TPS, Map sensor both quit. May have been coincedence but thats way it happened.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I was worried about this myself after I did it, which is why right after I am done, I start my car up and let most of it burn off.  Then I go out for a little drive, and by the time I come home, most of the armorall residue is gone, but the shine is still there!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||1
